  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Revenue against expenditure for a year. The money supply belongs to monetary policy, the balance of payments to external trade, and unemployment is an outcome rather than a budget item.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Revenue above spending. Expenditure above revenue is a deficit, equality is a balanced budget, and borrowing is one way of financing a deficit.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Spending above revenue. The reversal of this pair — deficit and surplus — is the single most common error in the topic.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The central bank. Borrowing from it creates new money rather than transferring existing savings, so it adds directly to the money supply.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It keeps the machine running rather than building anything. Capital expenditure creates assets such as roads, schools and power stations.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Spending and taxation. Interest rates and the money supply are monetary policy, exchange controls are trade policy, and wage guidelines are incomes policy.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Unemployment and recession, because the aim is to raise aggregate demand. Against high inflation the appropriate policy is the opposite one.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Higher taxes and lower spending both reduce aggregate demand. The other options are expansionary, and the last two are monetary rather than fiscal instruments.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It operates without a fresh decision: unemployment benefits rise and progressive tax receipts fall automatically when incomes drop, cushioning the fall.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The payer bears it and cannot shift it. A tax levied on goods is indirect, and one that can be shifted is by definition indirect.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Personal income tax is levied on income and cannot be passed on. VAT, import duty and excise duty are all levied on goods and are borne by consumers.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    VAT is charged on the sale of a good and passed to the buyer. The other three are levied on profits or gains and stay where they fall.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Progressive: the rate rises with income, so the share paid rises too. Proportional keeps the share constant, and regressive lets it fall.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Proportional. A flat rate on all income takes 10 per cent of ₦50,000 and 10 per cent of ₦500,000 — the same share, which is what the word means.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Regressive: 20 per cent of the smaller income and 5 per cent of the larger. A flat amount in naira is always regressive, however it is labelled.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Poor households spend a larger fraction of their income on goods, so the same rate bites harder. Who collects it is irrelevant to its incidence.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Equity. Certainty is about knowing what is owed, economy about collection costs, and convenience about the time and manner of payment.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Convenience: the tax is deducted at the moment the income is received, which suits the payer and reduces evasion.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Revenue falls, because work, trade and investment move out of the taxed sector. That is the whole content of the curve.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Who bears it. The legal payer and the economic bearer are frequently different people, and the difference is what incidence studies.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Less elastic, because that side cannot change quantity to escape the tax. Elasticity, not the statute, decides who pays.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Entirely by consumers: they will pay any price for the same quantity, so the whole tax passes forward into the price.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Entirely by producers, who cannot reduce quantity in response. This is the classic case of a fixed factor, such as land.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The accumulated stock of past borrowing. The deficit is the annual flow that adds to it, and reserves are an asset rather than a liability.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The payments due in the year — interest plus principal. It is the part of the debt that competes with schools and roads in the budget.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is owed in foreign currency, so repayment requires foreign exchange and a depreciation raises its domestic cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Government demand for credit pushes interest rates up, which prices private borrowers out of the market — the standard criticism of deficit financing domestically.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Company income tax is a receipt. Imports, unemployment and inflation are economic conditions, not revenue heads, although imports generate customs duty.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Raise revenue or cut expenditure. Printing money and borrowing widen the problem by adding to the money supply or to the debt stock.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Raise revenue. Redistribution and the regulation of behaviour are secondary purposes, and inflation control is a monetary matter.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Little revenue, because the quantity falls sharply when the price rises. Revenue is largest where demand is inelastic.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It reduces inequality. Ease of collection, revenue yield and incentives are separate arguments and are often made against progressivity rather than for it.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Proceeds from selling an asset are a capital receipt. The three taxes are recurrent revenue, which is collected year after year.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Government revenue, expenditure and debt. The others are private finance, which is studied separately.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    An indirect tax levied at the border and borne by consumers of the imported good, which is also why it is protection as well as revenue.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Producers absorb most of the tax, so the price rises little and the quantity falls sharply — the opposite of the inelastic case.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    January to December, which matters when a question dates a budget or a revenue figure.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A surplus with a falling debt ratio. The others either add to the debt or finance it in the most inflationary way available.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Evasion is illegal — not declaring income or falsifying returns. Avoidance uses legal means to reduce the liability, and the distinction is a favourite question.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Non-excludable and non-rival: no one can be kept out and one person's use does not reduce another's, so private sellers cannot charge and the state must provide.
  ]

]

